    {
      "id": "sbl8rxw324v59",
      "title": "SGLT1: why 2 Na+?",
      "createdAt": 1790825793000,
      "updatedAt": 1790825897430,
      "messages": [
        {
          "role": "user",
          "content": "why does SGLT1 need two sodium ions per glucose and not one?",
          "ts": 1790825826867
        },
        {
          "role": "assistant",
          "content": "Energy budget. Glucose in the enterocyte can be much more concentrated than in the lumen, so moving it in is uphill. Each Na+ moving in releases free energy (concentration gradient **plus** the negative membrane potential).\n\nOne Na+ only provides enough to concentrate glucose ~100-fold; **two** Na+ square that, allowing ~10 000-fold accumulation. That lets the gut absorb glucose almost completely even when lumen levels are low.\n\nThe notes don't derive this, so it's a good one to confirm in office hours as you planned.",
          "ts": 1790825897430
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
